# Predicting 30-day Hospital Readmission Among Diabetic Patients

## Project Overview

This project analyzes hospital encounter data from diabetic patients to identify factors associated with 30-day hospital readmission and build predictive models for readmission risk.

The workflow includes data cleaning, exploratory data analysis, feature encoding, logistic regression, random forest modeling, hyperparameter tuning, threshold adjustment, and model interpretation.

## Dataset

The original dataset included 101,766 hospital encounters and 50 variables. After missing value handling and data cleaning, the final analytic dataset included 98,053 encounters and 46 variables.

The outcome variable was converted into a binary indicator:

- `readmitted_30 = 1`: the patient was readmitted within 30 days
- `readmitted_30 = 0`: the patient was not readmitted within 30 days

Only about 11.3% of encounters resulted in 30-day readmission, making this an imbalanced classification problem.

## Methods

The analysis included:

- Missing value handling
- Exploratory data analysis
- One-hot encoding for categorical variables
- Train-test split with stratification
- Logistic Regression baseline model
- Random Forest model
- GridSearchCV hyperparameter tuning
- Threshold adjustment
- Feature importance analysis

## Key Findings

- The 30-day readmission rate was approximately 11.3%.
- Prior inpatient visits were strongly associated with 30-day readmission.
- Patients prescribed diabetes medication had a higher readmission rate than those who were not.
- Patients readmitted within 30 days had slightly higher medication burden.
- Time in hospital alone did not clearly separate readmitted and non-readmitted patients.

## Model Results

| Model | ROC-AUC | Precision for Readmission | Recall for Readmission | F1-score |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Logistic Regression | 0.632 | 0.17 | 0.49 | 0.25 |
| Random Forest | 0.653 | 0.18 | 0.55 | 0.27 |
| Tuned Random Forest | 0.656 | 0.19 | 0.46 | 0.27 |

The tuned Random Forest achieved the highest ROC-AUC of 0.656. However, the baseline Random Forest achieved the highest recall for identifying actual 30-day readmissions.

## Threshold Adjustment

Because the outcome was imbalanced, different classification thresholds were evaluated. Lowering the threshold to 0.4 increased recall to 0.749, allowing the model to identify about three-quarters of actual 30-day readmissions, although precision decreased.

